From 0000000000000000000000000000000000000000 Mon Sep 17 00:00:00 2001
From: Cloud Office <me@diegonmarcos.com>
Date: Thu, 1 Oct 2026 16:18:26 +0000
Subject: [PATCH] cloud-office: join the fleet mesh through the shared
 libs:core

Cloud Office is not a fleet mesh member. It is signed with the fleet key
and already crosses an application boundary (Text Enhance binds the
Cloud Keyboard over ITextTools), but its merged manifest carries none of
the five things membership is made of: the CONSTELLATION_DATA
<permission> and <uses-permission>, FleetTokenProvider at
<package>.fleet, the FleetMemberReceiver that answers MESH_MEMBER, and
the <queries> intent that lets it see the others. So SuperApp's Apps
Mesh page and /api/fleet/peers in every member leave it out, and it
serves no 380xx debug API, so a fault in it can only be guessed at.

Every other member gets all five the same way, and so does this one
now: by linking ab_cloud-libs-shared/libs/core, which declares the
permission and carries libs:devtools with an `api` dependency. Nothing
is copied - materialize links both directories into android/libs
(build.json::build.modules), exactly as it already links text-tools and
ml-l-image. libs:devtools reads the consuming app's build.json by
walking up from this gradle root, which sits in a scratch tree outside
the repository, so materialize links ac_cloud-office/build.json in as
android/build.json too; without that link the build stops at
configuration time.

The Gradle paths are fixed by core itself: it depends on
project(':libs:devtools'), which resolves against this settings.gradle,
so both must be included under those exact names. Placing them under
android/libs also gives the implicit ':libs' container a directory that
exists, which Gradle 9 (this tree pins 9.5.0) otherwise refuses.

Nothing else in the tree changes. core and devtools are Kotlin, built
by the Kotlin Gradle plugin that patch 0003 already hands Kotlin back
to; both declare minSdk 26, which is what android/app and android/lib
already declare; compileSdk 36 is already installed for text-tools.
---
 android/app/build.gradle |  4 ++++
 android/settings.gradle  | 12 +++++++++++-
 2 files changed, 15 insertions(+), 1 deletion(-)

diff --git a/android/app/build.gradle b/android/app/build.gradle
index 38802c2..fc8315c 100644
--- a/android/app/build.gradle
+++ b/android/app/build.gradle
@@ -85,6 +85,10 @@ dependencies {
     implementation 'androidx.preference:preference:1.2.1'
     implementation project(path: ':lib')
 
+    // #743: fleet mesh member. libs:core (+ devtools via `api`) merges the fleet provider,
+    // receiver, <queries> and the signature permission into this APK; nothing here calls it.
+    implementation project(':libs:core')
+
     // https://stackoverflow.com/questions/75263047/duplicate-class-in-kotlin-android
     constraints {
         implementation("org.jetbrains.kotlin:kotlin-stdlib-jdk7:1.8.0") {
diff --git a/android/settings.gradle b/android/settings.gradle
index b153f4b..421c19a 100644
--- a/android/settings.gradle
+++ b/android/settings.gradle
@@ -1,4 +1,4 @@
-include ':app', ':lib', ':text-tools', ':ml-l-image'
+include ':app', ':lib', ':text-tools', ':ml-l-image', ':libs:core', ':libs:devtools'
 
 // SHARED BY REFERENCE, not copied. ab_cloud-libs-shared/libs/text-tools is the ITextTools
 // binder contract and its client, and cloud-mail and the Cloud Keyboard already build the
@@ -16,3 +16,13 @@ project(':text-tools').projectDir = new File(settingsDir, 'text-tools')
 // a copy vendored in here would be the duplicate-engine defect this fleet fixed
 // (#170/#261). materialize places it at android/ml-l-image.
 project(':ml-l-image').projectDir = new File(settingsDir, 'ml-l-image')
+
+// #743 — fleet mesh membership. ab_cloud-libs-shared/libs/core, shared BY REFERENCE like
+// the two above, manifest-merges the CONSTELLATION_DATA signature permission (defined AND
+// requested) and, through its `api project(':libs:devtools')`, the fleet token provider,
+// the MESH_MEMBER receiver and its <queries>. The Gradle paths are not ours to choose:
+// core names devtools as ':libs:devtools', and that resolves against THIS file.
+// materialize places both under android/libs, which also gives the implicit ':libs'
+// container a real directory (Gradle 9 refuses one that does not exist).
+project(':libs:core').projectDir = new File(settingsDir, 'libs/core')
+project(':libs:devtools').projectDir = new File(settingsDir, 'libs/devtools')
